{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966813,"nonce":"LnRvlxHwlvcm0X/F6sKH6qrdIuc=","title":"html_view() widget — phased plan","message":"Build an `html_view(html_str)` widget that parses HTML5 and emits nodes directly into the existing layout tree (`RenderTree` / `LayoutNodeId` / taffy styles) — bypassing the `ElementBuilder` API. The widget owns a subtree of layout nodes scoped to its own bounds; the rest of the Blinc app is unchanged.\n\n**Why:**\n1. Enable webview-less platforms (headless Linux, minimal embedded, kiosks) to render HTML content in-app — docs, READMEs, emails, markdown output, embedded dashboards.\n2. The layout-tree-direct path is ~5× cheaper than HTML→ElementBuilder translation and gets us to ~85% HTML fidelity in 6-10 weeks. Blinc's `RenderTree` is already essentially a DOM + CSS — we leverage it directly.\n3. Blinc's existing `ColorSpan` / `PreparedText` multi-segment text pipeline (`blinc_text/src/renderer.rs`) handles inline styling (`\u003cb\u003e`, `\u003ci\u003e`, `\u003ca\u003e`, per-run color) without needing a full inline formatting context. That dissolves the biggest architectural blocker for 80% of real HTML (blog prose, docs, READMEs).\n4. **Most of the tag→widget mapping already exists.** `blinc_layout::widgets` and `blinc_cn::components` already cover ~80% of the semantic HTML tag set. Phase 1 is predominantly a walker that maps tags to existing widgets, not widget construction. Verify these mappings still hold against current code before starting — widget APIs drift.\n\n**How to apply:** Four phases below (plus Phase 0 prerequisites), each independently ships a usable subset. Before starting any phase, verify its assumptions still hold against current code — CSS parser, Stylesheet cascade, Text/ColorSpan APIs, and the existing widget catalog may have drifted. When scoping any HTML-related feature work, check which phase it belongs to and flag scope creep.\n\n---\n\n\nThis is the cheat sheet the Phase 1 walker consults. Verify each mapping still exists in current code before relying on it — widget file locations and function signatures drift.\n\n\n| HTML | Widget | File |\n|---|---|---|\n| `\u003cblockquote\u003e` | `blockquote` | `crates/blinc_layout/src/widgets/blockquote.rs` |\n| `\u003cbutton\u003e` | `button` | `widgets/button.rs` |\n| `\u003cinput type=\"checkbox\"\u003e` | `checkbox` | `widgets/checkbox.rs` |\n| `\u003ccode\u003e` / `\u003cpre\u003e` | `code` | `widgets/code.rs` |\n| `\u003chr\u003e` | `hr` | `widgets/hr.rs` |\n| `\u003ca href\u003e` | `link` | `widgets/link.rs` |\n| `\u003cul\u003e` / `\u003col\u003e` / `\u003cli\u003e` | `list` | `widgets/list.rs` |\n| `\u003cvideo\u003e` / `\u003caudio\u003e` | `media/` (with `controls`, `waveform`, `format_time`) | `widgets/media/` |\n| `\u003cinput type=\"radio\"\u003e` | `radio` | `widgets/radio.rs` |\n| `\u003ctable\u003e` / `\u003ctr\u003e` / `\u003ctd\u003e` | `table` | `widgets/table.rs` |\n| `\u003ctextarea\u003e` | `text_area` | `widgets/text_area.rs` |\n| `\u003cinput type=\"text\\|email\\|...\"\u003e` | `text_edit` / `text_input` | `widgets/text_edit.rs` |\n| scrolling container (overflow:scroll) | `scroll` | `widgets/scroll.rs` |\n| virtualized long list | `virtual_list` | `widgets/virtual_list.rs` |\n\n\n| HTML | Component | File |\n|---|---|---|\n| `\u003cdetails\u003e` / `\u003csummary\u003e` | `collapsible` / `collapsible_section` | `crates/blinc_cn/src/components/collapsible.rs` |\n| Multiple `\u003cdetails\u003e` grouped | `accordion` | `components/accordion.rs` |\n| `\u003cdialog\u003e` / `alert` dialog | `dialog` / `alert_dialog` | `components/dialog.rs` |\n| `\u003cprogress\u003e` | `progress` / `progress_animated` | `components/progress.rs` |\n| `\u003ckbd\u003e` | `kbd` | `components/kbd.rs` |\n| `\u003cinput list\u003e` + `\u003cdatalist\u003e` | `combobox` | `components/combobox.rs` |\n| `\u003cselect\u003e` / `\u003coption\u003e` | `select` | `components/select.rs` |\n| `\u003clabel for\u003e` | `label` | `components/label.rs` |\n| `\u003ch1\u003e`–`\u003ch6\u003e` / `\u003cp\u003e` | `h1..h6`, `heading(n)`, `p`, `muted`, `caption` | `components/typography.rs` |\n| `\u003cmenu\u003e` / `\u003cmenuitem\u003e` | `context_menu` + `menubar` + `dropdown_menu` + `navigation_menu` | `components/*_menu.rs` |\n| `\u003chr\u003e` styled | `separator` | `components/separator.rs` |\n| `\u003cinput type=\"range\"\u003e` | `slider` | `components/slider.rs` |\n| Tooltip / hover target | `tooltip` / `hover_card` | `components/tooltip.rs`, `components/hover_card.rs` |\n| `\u003caside\u003e` / drawer / sheet | `drawer` / `sheet` / `sidebar` | `components/drawer.rs`, `components/sheet.rs`, `components/sidebar.rs` |\n| Toggle control | `switch` | `components/switch.rs` |\n| Tabs | `tabs` | `components/tabs.rs` |\n| Tree view | `tree` | `components/tree.rs` |\n| Spinner / skeleton loaders | `spinner` / `skeleton` | `components/spinner.rs`, `components/skeleton.rs` |\n| Toast notification | `toast` | `components/toast.rs` |\n| Popover | `popover` | `components/popover.rs` |\n| Aspect-ratio container | `aspect_ratio` | `components/aspect_ratio.rs` |\n| Resizable panes | `resizable` | `components/resizable.rs` |\n\n\n- `\u003ccanvas\u003e` → `blinc_canvas_kit`\n- Inline `\u003csvg\u003e` → `blinc_svg`\n- Icon glyph (e.g. `\u003ci class=\"icon-*\"\u003e`) → `blinc_cn::icon` and `blinc_icons`\n\n---\n\n\nA handful of HTML tags have no direct widget equivalent today. These are small enough to build as standalone widgets AND become free coverage for the html_view walker. Ship them as part of `blinc_layout::widgets` or `blinc_cn::components` — they're useful on their own, not just for html_view.\n\n| HTML | Widget to build | Size | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `\u003cfigure\u003e` + `\u003cfigcaption\u003e` | `figure(media, caption)` | XS (1 day) | Div wrapping Image/Video/Canvas + Text caption with correct alignment and gap. Maps to a card pattern that blog/docs/gallery UIs already want. |\n| `\u003cmark\u003e` | `mark(text)` | XS (1 day) | Typography variant — Text with highlighted background + small padding. Reuse `blinc_cn::typography` layout. |\n| `\u003cabbr title\u003e` | `abbr(short, full)` | XS (1 day) | Thin wrapper over the existing `tooltip` component. Hover/focus reveals the full form. |\n| `\u003cmeter\u003e` | `meter(value, min, max, low?, high?, optimum?)` | S (2-3 days) | Different from `progress`: static gauge with color thresholds (green/yellow/red by value zone). Used for disk quota, battery, rating displays. |\n| `\u003cfieldset\u003e` + `\u003clegend\u003e` | `fieldset(legend, children)` | XS (1 day) | Grouping Div with 1px border + an inset title absolutely positioned in the top border gap. Every settings dialog wants this. |\n| `\u003cdl\u003e` + `\u003cdt\u003e` + `\u003cdd\u003e` | `definition_list([(term, description), ...])` | XS (1 day) | Two-column key/value layout via flex or grid. API docs, metadata panels. |\n| `\u003coutput\u003e` | `computed_output(formula_fn)` | S (2-3 days) | Reactive form output bound to one or more input signals. Natural fit with `blinc_core::reactive`. |\n| `\u003cpicture\u003e` + `\u003csource\u003e` | `responsive_image(sources)` | S (2-3 days) | Picks best source by widget bounds / DPR / supported format. Can leverage `aspect_ratio`. |\n\n**Success criteria:** Each widget lands in the normal example gallery AND has a 5-10 line unit test demonstrating the rendered output is correct at default styling.\n\n**Don't block Phase 1 on all of them**: the walker can emit a generic styled Div for unsupported tags and a Phase 0 widget swap-in is a one-line change.\n\n---\n\n\n`html_view` is a **Stateful** widget, not a pure function. The state container owns parser state, the parsed intermediate DOM, form values, the render queue, and the callbacks. This is non-negotiable — a pure-function `html_view(\u0026str)` would lose form input values, scroll position, focus, and in-progress CSS animations on every parent rebuild. See the \"State ownership \u0026 render queue\" section below for the full contract.\n\n**Scope:**\n- `html_view(\u0026state)` or `html_view_from_string(html_str)` (the second internally creates a private state) returns a builder that emits a Stateful container.\n- Integrate `html5ever` as DOM parser, fed through the Stateful's `HtmlViewState`.\n- Walk the state's parsed DOM → emit layout nodes into the Stateful's body using a tag→widget lookup table (see inventory above). This is predominantly plumbing, not new widget code:\n  - Structural blocks (`\u003cdiv\u003e`, `\u003csection\u003e`, `\u003carticle\u003e`, `\u003cheader\u003e`, `\u003cfooter\u003e`, `\u003cnav\u003e`, `\u003cmain\u003e`, `\u003caside\u003e`) → `Div` with the HTML5 default margin/padding\n  - `\u003ch1\u003e-\u003ch6\u003e` / `\u003cp\u003e` / `\u003cblockquote\u003e` → `blinc_cn::typography::{h1..h6, p}` / `blockquote`\n  - `\u003cpre\u003e` / `\u003ccode\u003e` → `code` widget\n  - `\u003cimg\u003e` → Image layout node (existing pipeline)\n  - `\u003cul\u003e/\u003col\u003e/\u003cli\u003e` → `list` widget\n  - `\u003chr\u003e` → `hr` widget\n  - `\u003ca href\u003e` → `link` widget\n- Inline tags collapse into `ColorSpan`s on the parent block's Text:\n  - `\u003cb\u003e` / `\u003cstrong\u003e` → bold color span (or font-weight span when bold weight registration lands)\n  - `\u003ci\u003e` / `\u003cem\u003e` → italic span\n  - `\u003ca\u003e` (inside a text flow) → link-colored + underlined span + click hit region\n  - `\u003cspan\u003e` → color/styling span per inline `style` attribute\n- Build a minimal user-agent stylesheet (margin, font-size, display, list-style defaults) baked into the widget crate.\n- Emit unknown tags as a generic Div with a `tracing::warn!` so gaps surface during testing.\n- Assign deterministic stable ids to every emitted layout node — required for form/scroll/animation persistence (see below).\n\n**Success criteria:** Render a well-formed blog post (400-word article with headings, paragraphs, inline bold/italic/links, one embedded image, one list) with visually correct output against the same HTML in Firefox. Typing into an `\u003cinput\u003e` inside the widget and having the parent component rebuild for any reason must not clear the input.\n\n---\n\n\n`html_view` wraps its body in a Stateful container. The state struct owns everything that must survive parent rebuilds — parser handle, accumulated DOM, pending CSS, form values, render queue flags. This is the single most important architectural decision for the widget: without it, streaming is impossible and user interactions inside the html_view lose state on every parent rebuild.\n\n\n```rust\npub struct HtmlViewState {\n    // Parser — html5ever's Parser has a feed() method for streaming.\n    // None once parsing completes.\n    parser: Option\u003chtml5ever::driver::Parser\u003cParsedDom\u003e\u003e,\n    parse_complete: bool,\n    head_flushed: bool,\n\n    // Intermediate DOM — a Rust-side tree that the walker transforms\n    // into Blinc Divs on each rebuild. Cheaper than re-parsing HTML\n    // every rebuild, and allows the walker to assign stable node ids.\n    dom: ParsedDom,\n\n    // Stylesheet accumulated from \u003cstyle\u003e tags. Buffered until head\n    // is flushed, then applied wholesale to prevent FOUC.\n    pending_stylesheet: String,\n\n    // Form field values — keyed by the stable node id of the input.\n    // Survives rebuilds because the walker hands out the same id.\n    form_values: HashMap\u003cString, FormValue\u003e,\n\n    // Render queue — coalesces bursts of parser events into\n    // frame-aligned flushes. Without this, a streaming parser can\n    // trigger hundreds of rebuilds per second.\n    dirty: bool,\n    last_flush: web_time::Instant,\n    flush_budget_ms: u64,   // ~16ms default\n\n    // User callbacks\n    on_navigate: Arc\u003cdyn Fn(\u0026str) + Send + Sync\u003e,\n    on_submit:   Arc\u003cdyn Fn(\u0026HashMap\u003cString, FormValue\u003e) + Send + Sync\u003e,\n}\n```\n\n\nEvery DOM node the walker emits gets a deterministic id computed from its path in the parsed tree — `\"blinc-html-{depth}-{sibling_index}\"` or a stable hash of the ancestor chain. Appending new content at the bottom of the document does not shift existing ids. This one choice gives us **four kinds of state persistence for free**, because Blinc's existing subsystems already key their state on layout node ids:\n\n- **Scroll position** survives rebuilds — the scroll widget keys its offset on the owner id.\n- **Form input values** survive — `text_edit` / `checkbox` / `radio` state is keyed on the owner id. The walker just passes the stable id through when emitting form nodes.\n- **CSS animations and transitions in progress** survive — `RenderTree::css_animations` / `css_transitions` HashMaps are keyed on `LayoutNodeId`, which stays stable.\n- **Focus** survives — the event router's focused-node state is keyed the same way.\n\nIf the walker ever changes its id scheme (e.g. a bug that makes ids non-deterministic), every one of these will break silently. Write a test that parses the same HTML twice in a row and asserts all emitted ids are identical.\n\n\nhtml5ever can emit thousands of events per chunk during a burst. Marking dirty on every event and rebuilding the Blinc tree each time would be wasteful. The state batches events internally and only toggles its dirty signal at frame-aligned boundaries OR at natural flush points:\n\n- End of a block element (close of `\u003c/p\u003e`, `\u003c/li\u003e`, `\u003c/div\u003e`, etc.)\n- End of `\u003chead\u003e` (the FOUC-avoidance flush — body rendering begins only after head is fully parsed and the accumulated stylesheet has been applied)\n- End of input (the streaming source closes)\n- `flush_budget_ms` elapsed since last flush (default 16ms for 60fps cadence)\n\nThe Blinc frame loop's existing dirty-check then triggers at most one rebuild per frame regardless of how many parser events arrived.\n\n\nA 1MB HTML document parses to roughly 10,000 DOM nodes. Rebuilding 10,000 Blinc Divs per frame is ~5-15ms of `from_element_with_registry` + taffy work depending on hardware. Tolerable but not free. Two mitigations if it becomes a bottleneck:\n\n1. **Bounded rebuild via cursor**: the state tracks a `last_flushed_node_id` cursor. On rebuild, only the subtree under the last-mutated ancestor walks — the rest is detected as unchanged via the stable ids and reused. Most HTML documents append content at the tail, so the bounded region is small in practice.\n2. **Stateful subtree pinning** (framework change, defer until measured): let Stateful widgets opt into \"my subtree doesn't rebuild unless I mark it dirty\". The parent rebuild flows around them entirely. Worth a separate architectural discussion only if rebuild cost is actually measured as a problem. Not required for Phase 1.\n\n\n`HtmlViewState::new` accepts one of:\n\n```rust\npub enum HtmlSource {\n    String(String),                                    // sync, one-shot\n    Bytes(Vec\u003cu8\u003e),                                    // sync, one-shot\n    Stream(Pin\u003cBox\u003cdyn AsyncRead + Send\u003e\u003e),            // Phase 1.5\n    Feed,                                              // Phase 1.5 — external code pushes via state.feed(bytes)\n}\n```\n\nPhase 1 ships `String` and `Bytes` only. Streaming sources land in Phase 1.5 once the Stateful container is proven out.\n\n---\n\n\nPhase 1's state container already owns the parser handle and render queue, so streaming is largely just switching the input source. html5ever's tokenizer is natively streaming-capable (Servo uses it this way) — feed bytes in chunks via the parser's `process` method, it emits DOM events incrementally without waiting for the byte stream to close.\n\n**Scope:**\n- Extend `HtmlSource` with `Stream(AsyncRead)` and `Feed` variants.\n- `Feed` mode: external code (async fetcher, WebSocket, file reader) calls `state.feed(\u0026[u8])` on each chunk. The state forwards to its parser, debounces dirty via `flush_budget_ms`, and the frame loop picks up from there.\n- `Stream` mode: the state spawns an internal async task (via wasm-bindgen-futures on web, tokio/smol on native) that pumps the async reader into `feed()`.\n- **FOUC avoidance**: body rendering is deferred until `head_flushed = true`. Until then, the walker emits nothing into the Blinc tree even if body tokens have already arrived. `\u003cstyle\u003e` tags inside `\u003chead\u003e` are accumulated into `pending_stylesheet` and applied wholesale when head closes. This matches browser behavior and is the primary reason for the head/body split state.\n- **`\u003cstyle\u003e` mid-body**: rare but possible. Simple approach — append the rule to the active stylesheet and mark the entire html_view subtree dirty so the next frame re-applies CSS to existing nodes. Expensive but rare enough that the cost is acceptable.\n\n**Success criteria:** Stream a 1MB HTML document from a slow source (simulated 100KB/sec) and see progressive rendering — the first paragraph appears within ~200ms, subsequent content flows in as bytes arrive, scroll position stays stable as content appends below the viewport, and typing into a form input mid-stream does not get clobbered by new parse events.\n\n---\n\n\n**Scope:**\n- Extend `blinc_layout::css_parser` with:\n  - Specificity computation `(a, b, c)` stored per rule; sort rules by specificity + source order\n  - `!important` flag on declarations, overrides normal specificity\n  - Attribute selectors: `[href]`, `[type=\"text\"]`, `[class~=\"foo\"]`, `[class^=\"bar\"]`, `[class$=\"-active\"]`, `[class*=\"dis\"]`\n  - `calc()` expression evaluator (supports `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, nested, unit arithmetic)\n  - `var(--name, fallback)` custom property substitution (already partially present for theme tokens — verify and extend)\n  - `@media` rules evaluated against the **widget's content bounds**, not the OS viewport; re-evaluate on widget resize\n  - Viewport units (`vw`, `vh`, `vmin`, `vmax`) resolved against widget bounds, not screen\n- Parse and honor `\u003cstyle\u003e` tags in the HTML input\n- Parse `\u003clink rel=\"stylesheet\"\u003e` — requires a fetch callback the user supplies (no default network I/O inside the widget)\n\n**Success criteria:** Render a page with a non-trivial stylesheet (specificity-dependent rules, `!important` overrides, `@media (max-width: 600px)` breakpoints, CSS custom properties for theming) correctly at multiple widget sizes.\n\n---\n\n\n**Scope:**\n- Map HTML form elements to existing Blinc widgets emitted as layout nodes:\n  - `\u003cinput type=\"text|email|password|number|tel|url\"\u003e` → `text_edit` layout node\n  - `\u003cinput type=\"checkbox\"\u003e` → `checkbox` layout node\n  - `\u003cinput type=\"radio\"\u003e` → `radio` layout node\n  - `\u003cinput type=\"range\"\u003e` → `slider` layout node\n  - `\u003cinput list\u003e` + `\u003cdatalist\u003e` → `combobox` layout node\n  - `\u003ctextarea\u003e` → `text_area` layout node\n  - `\u003cselect\u003e/\u003coption\u003e` → `select` layout node\n  - `\u003cbutton\u003e` → `button` layout node with existing hit-test\n  - `\u003clabel for=\"id\"\u003e` → wired to the target input's focus via `blinc_cn::label`\n  - `\u003cfieldset\u003e` + `\u003clegend\u003e` → the Phase 0 `fieldset` widget\n  - `\u003coutput\u003e` → the Phase 0 `computed_output` widget\n- `\u003cform\u003e` submission hook: user supplies a `on_submit(form_data)` callback; widget gathers named inputs and invokes it (no network — user decides what to do with the data)\n- `\u003ca href=\"...\"\u003e` click → user-supplied `on_navigate(url)` callback (widget doesn't do its own routing)\n- Validation attributes (`required`, `min`, `max`, `pattern`, `type=\"email\"`) surface as pre-submit checks\n\n**Success criteria:** Render and interact with a typical HTML form (login: email + password + remember checkbox + submit; contact: text fields + textarea + select + submit) and capture submission via the callback.\n\n---\n\n\nSmaller than originally scoped because `\u003ctable\u003e`, `\u003cul\u003e`/`\u003col\u003e`, `\u003cdetails\u003e`, and `\u003cdialog\u003e` already have widgets — the walker just wires them up.\n\n**Scope:**\n- `\u003ctable\u003e` / `\u003cthead\u003e` / `\u003ctbody\u003e` / `\u003ctfoot\u003e` / `\u003ctr\u003e` / `\u003ctd\u003e` / `\u003cth\u003e` → `blinc_layout::widgets::table` (or taffy grid fallback if the widget can't express colspan/rowspan cleanly)\n- `colspan` / `rowspan` → grid spanning cells or `table` widget extension\n- `\u003cul\u003e` / `\u003col\u003e` with `list-style-type` (disc, circle, square, decimal, lower-alpha, upper-alpha, lower-roman, upper-roman) — either honored by `list` widget or emitted as a leading text marker\n- `\u003cdetails\u003e` / `\u003csummary\u003e` → `blinc_cn::collapsible`\n- `\u003cdialog\u003e` → `blinc_cn::dialog`\n- `\u003cdl\u003e` / `\u003cdt\u003e` / `\u003cdd\u003e` → the Phase 0 `definition_list` widget\n- `\u003cfigure\u003e` / `\u003cfigcaption\u003e` → the Phase 0 `figure` widget\n- HTML entity decoding (already present via `html_escape` in `blinc_text` — reuse)\n\n**Success criteria:** Render a typical email newsletter (table-based layout with colspan, nested lists, inline styles) at 85%+ fidelity against the same email in a real client.\n\n---\n\n\n- **True inline formatting context** — mid-line inline images, different font sizes per line, ruby, vertical-align across inline siblings. The ColorSpan approach from Phase 1 handles same-font-size inline styling but not size-mixing. A real inline context is its own 6-8 week project; revisit once Phases 1-4 ship.\n- **CSS Grid via user stylesheets** — taffy supports it but exposing it through `blinc_layout::css_parser` is a separate ~8 week effort. Phase 4's table rendering uses grid internally but the feature isn't exposed for author CSS.\n- **Float layout** — `float: left/right` with text-wrap-around. Rarely used in modern HTML; defer until a real user needs it.\n- **JavaScript** — explicitly never in scope. The widget renders static HTML + CSS. If users want interactivity, they bind callbacks on the widget side.\n- **Writing modes** — `writing-mode: vertical-rl` and friends. Not needed for the target use cases.\n- **@counter-style, @font-face** — counter-style is a rare feature. `@font-face` is tempting but blocked on network fetching policy.\n- **Print layout** — `@page`, page-break-*. Never needed for a webview-replacement use case.\n- **Shadow DOM / custom elements** — not in the HTML spec surface we care about.\n\n\n- **Prototype recommendation (do this first)**: a standalone 100-200 line walker that takes a single blog post, emits layout nodes into a Stateful container, and renders once. Purpose is to validate (a) the ColorSpan-for-inline-styling assumption, (b) that the existing widget catalog renders under walker-emitted nodes as cleanly as it does under `ElementBuilder` use, and (c) that stable node ids survive a parent rebuild (type into an `\u003cinput\u003e`, trigger a parent rebuild, confirm the input keeps its value). If all three hold, Phases 1-4 are plumbing. If they don't, re-scope.\n- **Revised total estimate**: 8-11 weeks for 85% HTML fidelity on typical well-formed HTML documents, including Phase 1.5 streaming. Phase 0 prerequisites run in parallel with Phase 1 and don't extend the critical path.\n- **Separate from the 7-9 month \"general HTML renderer\" estimate** — that earlier scope was wrong-framed (HTML→ElementBuilder instead of HTML→layout tree) AND didn't credit the existing widget catalog.\n- **Success metric**: pick ~20 real-world HTML documents (blog posts, docs sites, READMEs, emails, forms) and measure \"render correctly without manual tweaking\" as a pass-rate. Target 18/20 by end of Phase 4. For Phase 1.5 specifically, add a streaming-specific test: stream the largest document from the test set at throttled rates (100KB/sec, 1MB/sec, 10MB/sec) and verify progressive rendering, scroll stability, and form-input persistence.\n- **Widget catalog drift**: the inventory table above is a snapshot. Re-verify widget file paths and public signatures before writing the walker's tag mapping — `blinc_cn` and `blinc_layout::widgets` evolve faster than this memory.\n- **Stable id determinism test**: write a regression test early in Phase 1 that parses the same HTML twice in a row and asserts every emitted `LayoutNodeId` matches. Id non-determinism is a silent-failure bug class — every state persistence claim in the plan depends on it holding.","files":null}]}